\details{
Generates wide-format survival data (one row per individual) with up to 5 date
variables (entry, event, death, loss-to-follow-up, administrative censoring).
Applies temporal ordering constraints and supports garbage data generation for
QA testing.

This function implements v0.3.0 "recodeflow pattern" API:
\itemize{
\item Accepts full metadata data frames (not pre-filtered subsets)
\item Accepts variable names (not variable rows)
\item Filters metadata internally using databaseStart column
}

\strong{Implementation strategy:}
\enumerate{
\item Call create_date_var() once for each non-NULL date variable
\item Each variable configured separately in variables.csv with own event_prop,
distribution, followup_min, followup_max, etc.
\item Combine results into single data frame
\item Apply temporal ordering constraints
}

\strong{Temporal ordering constraints (normal mode):}
\itemize{
\item Entry date is always baseline (earliest)
\item All other dates must be >= entry_date
\item Death can occur before any event
\item If death < event, set event to NA (censored, not missing)
\item Observation ends at min(event, death, ltfu, admin_censor)
}

\strong{Temporal violations for QA testing (v0.3.0+):}
This function creates clean, temporally-ordered survival data. To generate
temporal violations for testing data quality pipelines:
\itemize{
\item Add \code{garbage_high_prop} and \code{garbage_high_range} to individual date variables
in variables.csv (e.g., future death dates beyond 2025)
\item Use \code{\link[=create_date_var]{create_date_var()}} to generate date variables with garbage
\item Test your temporal validation logic separately
\item This approach separates concerns: date-level garbage vs. survival data generation
}

